<p>
    Every time that you look up a video, look up an article, or check a map, you use a
    website. A website is a set of files. A web browser is a program that
    reads those files and show them to you.
</p>

<h2>What happens when you search up a website</h2>
<ol>
    <li>You type a <strong>URL</strong>, into your browser.</li>
    <li>Your browser sends a request to a server over the internet for the page.</li>
    <li>A server which is a computer that holds data sends files back to your browser</li>
    <li>Your browser then uses the files and shows the page on your screen.</li>
</ol>

<p class="note">
    <strong>So,</strong>after typing the url into your browser it sends a request
    and a server sends files to run the website.
</p>

<h2>This website is built with three kinds of code:</h2>

<table>
    <tr><td>HTML</td><td>Controls what is on the page</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CSS</td><td>Controls how the page looks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>C#</td><td>Builds the page on the server</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Static and dynamic pages</h2>
<p>
    A static page is the same for every time, A dynamic
    page is changed every time someone visits, like a scoreboard or the time.
</p>
<p>
    This website is dynamic. The home page lists @LessonData.All.Count parts, but that number was not typed into the page.
     C# the programming language makes it from a list of parts in the code.
</p>

<h2>Where is a website stored?</h2>
<p>
    Websites are usually stored in servers in data centers. However unpublished ones like this are stored on this computer instead of a
    data center.
</p>
